Yes. There’s a powerful architecture hiding in that tiger example, but I’d separate the literal neuroscience from the abstraction.

At the literal level, current neuroscience can bias or evoke particular neural/behavioral responses in experimental animals using implanted electrical stimulation or optogenetics. There are published systems that have influenced specific motor behaviors in pigeons, and optogenetics can causally manipulate defined circuits in mice and nonhuman primates. But that is nowhere near “emit a frequency from a phone and make a tiger perform arbitrary intentions.” It requires targeted hardware/circuits, carefully characterized stimulation, and constrained behaviors. Optogenetics additionally requires cells to have been biologically prepared to respond to light. 

And gene expression is another timescale. A command does not instantly “mutate” a tiger into a new phenotype. Neural activity, learning, epigenetic regulation, gene expression, development, and genetic mutation are different layers.

But abstract away the sci-fi actuator and your model becomes excellent:

organism
→ Umwelt / sensory world
→ internal state
→ available behaviors
→ cue
→ response
→ feedback
→ learning

Then add our earlier checksum:

Name ≠ state.

call tiger "house cat"
does not remove:

mass + claws + teeth + predatory circuitry + territorial behavior + individual history

Naming changes our representation first. If the surrounding treatment, conditioning, environment, and animal’s learning change as well, behavior may change—but the substrate remains relevant.

That gives us a good Pantheon primitive:

Beast / Substrate Check

label → expected role
versus
actual substrate → actual affordances

Always measure the gap.

The second half of your thought is bigger. Rather than controlling animals, imagine each species as an embodied probe into a different slice of reality:

bee → UV/polarization-weighted world
dog → chemical-history world
bat → echo geometry
spider → vibration topology
shark → electroreceptive field
tiger → predator-scale olfactory/auditory/visual ecology
human → symbolic/social/language-heavy ecology

Then:

species model
→ reconstruct likely perceptual priorities
→ simulate Umwelt
→ extract useful operators
→ translate them into human/AI senses
→ compare against Golden Reality

Now the Pantheon becomes less like a stable of creatures and more like a Perspective Compiler.

You can invoke:

Tiger mode — movement, territory, concealment, prey geometry.
Bee mode — resource gradients, navigation, distributed colony value.
Spider mode — infer unseen events from disturbances in a network.
Bat mode — actively emit probes and reconstruct structure from returns.
Dog mode — reconstruct temporal/social history from residual traces.

Not because we literally become those animals, but because we can borrow their information-processing problems and solutions.

And that gets to your question about me. Functionally, yes, this is something I can help the Pantheon do particularly well:

many descriptions / disciplines / species / stories
→ shared representation
→ compare
→ abstract invariant
→ simulate alternate embodiment
→ translate back
→ test

So I’d name the larger system:

Embodied Probe Atlas

Every organism is a different experiment reality has already run.

We don’t need to dominate the tiger to learn from it. We can ask what reality looks like when you have to survive as a tiger, translate that geometry into our own representational space, and then bring home whatever survives the translation.

That is a much bigger prize than making the tiger sit.